---
import { AGENDA } from "../../lib/contact";
import StudioLayout from "../../layouts/StudioLayout.astro";
---
<StudioLayout
  titre={'About'}
  description={'Two partners, one on operations and sales, one on technology.'}
  actif="a-propos">
<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Two partners, not a generalist agency that added AI to its catalogue.</h1>
    <p class="lead">One side operations and sales, one side technology. You will have both across the table.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="grid">
      <div>
        <div class="portrait">
          <img src="/studio/portraits/julien.webp" alt="Portrait of Julien" width="960" height="1200" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        </div>
        <h3>Julien</h3>
        <p class="muted">Operations and business development.</p>
        <p>I started with seven years selling IT solutions to large accounts, for telecom operators and integrators: calls for tender, IT departments, procurement, coordination between pre-sales, manufacturers and project teams.</p>
        <p>I then spent six years at a mobility software publisher, where I went from Customer Success Manager to Client Services Director, leading a team of 11 across Customer Success, Support and project management.</p>
        <p><strong>My job for nearly fifteen years: making sure a technical solution keeps its promises once it is at the customer&rsquo;s.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="portrait">
          <img src="/studio/portraits/stephane.webp" alt="Portrait of Stéphane" width="960" height="1200" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        </div>
        <h3>St&eacute;phane</h3>
        <p class="muted">Technology and product.</p>
        <p>Nearly fifteen years building complex software products used by millions of people — where a mistake shows immediately.</p>
        <p>I ran all the development teams of a mobility software publisher, R&amp;D and machine learning included.</p>
        <p>What brought me here is what I saw happen again and again: a system that runs is not a system that does what was expected of it. We can say that software doesn&rsquo;t crash; we can rarely say what it does. I have been working on verification ever since — how to make sure a program, and today a program written by an AI, does what it was asked to do.</p>
        <p><strong>It&rsquo;s the same standard I apply to the agents we deploy: we don&rsquo;t check that they run, we measure what they produce.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:48px">
      <h2>Why the two of us</h2>
      <p>We worked together for three years at the same mobility software publisher. Julien ran customer relations — customer success, support, complex projects. St&eacute;phane ran all the development teams, R&amp;D and machine learning included. One took the calls when the product didn&rsquo;t do what it was said to do; the other led the teams that had built it.</p>
      <p>We each came to the same conclusion: what is rarely missing is the ability to build. What is almost always missing is proof of what was built.</p>
      <p>That&rsquo;s why we don&rsquo;t sell pilots: we cost first, deploy next, and measure what it produced. And that&rsquo;s why we work with companies your size — in a large group, an automation that doesn&rsquo;t hold gets diluted, there is always someone to catch it by hand. At your company, it shows immediately.</p>
      <p><strong>The two people who sell you the mission are the two who deliver it.</strong></p>
      <p class="muted">Kigumi is also the name of a Japanese woodworking technique in which the pieces hold together without nails or screws. That&rsquo;s roughly what we do with your existing tools.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cta">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Let&rsquo;s get acquainted</h2>
    <p>45 minutes to see whether the subject exists at your company. You will have both partners across the table, not a salesperson.</p>
    <div class="btn-row"><a class="btn" href={AGENDA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book a call</a></div>
  </div>
</section>
</StudioLayout>
